# Prestium Tor configuration (on top of /usr/share/tor/tor-service-defaults-torrc)
#
# A SocksPort line here replaces every SocksPort of the defaults file, so the
# world-writable unix socket /run/tor/socks (which the per-uid firewall can't
# filter) is gone. Streams to different destination hosts/ports use separate
# circuits; Tor Browser isolates further by SOCKS username (first-party domain).
SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9050 IsolateDestAddr IsolateDestPort IsolateSOCKSAuth
# 2.0 r9: Tor Browser runs in a sandbox with no network at all (its own empty network namespace,
# like Tails' "tbb" namespace but without even a veth); this Unix socket, bound into the sandbox, is
# its only way out. Only members of prestium-tbb (the desktop user) can use it, the same users the
# firewall lets reach 9050. Tor Browser isolates by SOCKS username itself (IsolateSOCKSAuth is Tor's default).
# 2.0 r17: WorldWritable, not GroupWritable. With GroupWritable, Tor requires the directory's group
# to be its own (debian-tor) or root and silently skipped this socket because /run/prestium-tbb is
# group prestium-tbb (seen on the r16 ISO: 9050 open, no socket). Access is enforced by the
# directory instead: /run/prestium-tbb is 2750 debian-tor:prestium-tbb (tmpfiles.d), so only Tor and
# members of prestium-tbb can reach the socket, the same as Debian's /run/tor/socks model.
# 2.1: ExtendedErrors (as Tor Browser's own launcher sets): Tor tells the browser *why* an onion
# failed (F4 = needs a client key). Without it Tor Browser never showed its key prompt, so private
# OnionShare addresses (the default) failed with "Unable to connect". KeepAliveIsolateSOCKSAuth as well.
SocksPort unix:/run/prestium-tbb/socks WorldWritable ExtendedErrors KeepAliveIsolateSOCKSAuth
# no DNSPort/TransPort: nothing on Prestium resolves names outside Tor/I2P
AvoidDiskWrites 1
# Sandbox 1 removed: Tor refuses managed proxies (ClientTransportPlugin: obfs4/snowflake/webtunnel) in Sandbox mode, so tor@default failed. Tails does not use Sandbox either.
# IPv6 is disabled on Prestium
ClientUseIPv6 0
ClientPreferIPv6ORPort 0

# 2.1: IRC (Tor), The Lounge's Tor instance. These names reach each network's own onion service, so
# the server's TLS certificate (made for the name) checks out and nothing goes through an exit relay.
# The mappings and onion addresses are the networks' own (libera.chat/guides/connect,
# hackint.org/transport/tor, alphachat.net/tor.xhtml, pirateirc.net/servers.html). MapAddress applies
# to every SOCKS client of this Tor (Tor Browser too): there these names also lead to the same
# networks' official onion services, which is harmless.
MapAddress palladium.libera.chat libera75jm6of4wxpxt4aynol3xjmbtxgfyjpu34ss4d7r7q2v5zrpyd.onion
MapAddress guybrush.hackint.org dtlbunzs5b7s5sl775quwezleyeplxzicdoh3cnhm7feolxmkfd42nqd.onion
MapAddress irc.alphachat.net rnjlael46gqqmfiuiqsswdeev6axrf47j7bq4dtuqdsv2qgqfumcy4yd.onion
MapAddress roubaix-fr.pirateirc.net tll4bxf546kzf6iv4n2m4pbbjnifrfewe3kcritva2tuuuiowygx2cqd.onion

# Pluggable transports: obfs4 (lyrebird), Snowflake and WebTunnel are set up by the Tor Connection
# Assistant (menu: Network). Snowflake/meek/WebTunnel host names are resolved with DNS-over-HTTPS
# through the I2P outproxy and pinned for the transports; Tor traffic itself never uses I2P.

# Prestium 2.0: pluggable transports, bridges and UseBridges live in the Tor Connection
# Assistant's drop-in (/etc/tor/torrc.d/50-prestium-assistant.conf; default = direct connection).
%include /etc/tor/torrc.d/*.conf
